<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Deliberately NOT in Bunyi.sln. This reads the Windows UI Automation tree of
    a running Bunyi, so it is Windows-only (net10.0-windows) and would break the
    Linux half of a solution build. `dotnet.yml` builds it on the Windows runner
    so it cannot rot; nothing runs it there, because it needs a real desktop
    session and a real window. See tools/UiaProbe/README.md.

    Two UI Automation clients, on purpose, because neither can do the whole job:

      * the managed one (System.Windows.Automation, which UseWPF brings in) for
        walking the tree and reading names, types and accelerators. Pleasant to
        use and enough for most of the checks.

      * the COM one (IUIAutomation, generated by CsWin32) for the two things the
        managed client cannot do at all, both measured here rather than assumed:
        reading UIA_LiveSettingPropertyId (30135), where it hands back an
        ArgumentException object reading "Unsupported Property", and receiving
        UIA_LiveRegionChangedEventId (20024), where it accepts the subscription
        and then delivers nothing. Those two are exactly #192's question, so the
        second client is not optional.

    CsWin32 is a compile-time source generator (PrivateAssets=all): it adds a
    file of interop declarations to this tool's build and no dependency to
    anything the app ships. Nothing in src/ references it.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <AssemblyName>Bunyi.UiaProbe</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Bunyi.UiaProbe</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.CsWin32" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
